 ## Overview
-Hybrid-memory compositing is a parallel image assembly strategy that reduces communication overhead by aggregating ray-casting fragments in shared memory before exchanging them across the network. It solves the bottleneck of high message counts in distributed-memory compositing by allowing threads within a node to merge data locally, thereby sending fewer, larger blocks to the compositing tasks [S1]. It differs from pure distributed-memory approaches by leveraging intra-node shared memory to reduce the number of MPI messages by approximately 6x [S1].
